            "value": "COMMENTARY",
            "readOnly": true,
            "bold": true
          },
          "A21": {
            "value": "The 5% terminal growth rate balances Asian Paints' premium category positioning against macroeconomic reality: above India's 4% inflation floor but well below a 10% GDP growth ceiling that would be unsustainable for a mature ₹66,000 cr business. The 20x exit multiple reflects peer benchmarks from Sherwin-Williams (traded at 20-21x EBITDA) and AkzoNobel (18-22x), providing external discipline to terminal-year valuation. However, the two methods diverge markedly because Gordon Growth mathematically implies a 9.78x exit multiple—well below the 18-22x peer range—signalling that either perpetuity growth of 5% is conservative for Asian Paints' structural advantages (brand, pricing power in decorative paints) or WACC of 11.65% embeds too much equity-risk premium. TV contribution of 67-82% across methods underscores the model's inevitable sensitivity to terminal assumptions; small changes in g or exit multiple swing fair value by ±₹30-40 cr. A defensible published range of ₹120,000–₹145,000 cr acknowledges both the upside from multiple expansion as Asian Paints matures internationally and the downside from mean reversion in operating margins under competitive pressure.",
            "readOnly": true
